<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hathor</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hathor was one of the principal goddesses of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, associated with the sky, love, music, motherhood and foreign lands, and often shown as a cow or a woman with cow horns and a sun disc. Her main cult centre was Dendera. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> identified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalat-gebal">Baalat Gebal</a>, the goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, with Hathor, who was worshipped as 'Lady of Byblos' and of the turquoise mines of Sinai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hathor.jpg" alt="Hathor" width="320" height="754" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeff Dahl · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hathor.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">Cattle</a> imagery is frequent in the art of Predynastic Egypt, as are images of women with upraised, curved arms like horns, and both may represent goddesses connected with cattle. The Gerzeh Palette, from the Naqada II period, shows a cow's head with inward-curving horns surrounded by stars, which suggests a link with the sky.</p><p>Hathor herself is not unambiguously mentioned or depicted until the Fourth Dynasty, c. 2613 to 2494 BCE, although some objects that refer to her may be earlier. When she does clearly appear, her horns curve outward. Her Egyptian name, ḥwt-ḥr, is usually translated as house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, but it can also mean my house is the sky.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Egyptologist Robyn Gillam calls Hathor a type of deity rather than a single entity, and suggests that the royal goddess promoted by the Old Kingdom court absorbed many local goddesses, who were then treated as her manifestations. Texts speak of Seven Hathors, and sometimes of as many as 362.</p><p>As a sky goddess she was mother or consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> and Ra, and so the symbolic mother of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>. She was also one of the goddesses who acted as the Eye of Ra. Her other concerns were music, dance, love, sexuality and motherhood, and the sistrum, a rattle, was important in her worship. The Seven Hathors appear in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> tales at births, foretelling how the characters will die.</p><p>She was shown as a cow, as a woman with a cow's head, and most often as a woman wearing a headdress of cow horns and a sun disk. Hathor also helped the dead cross into the Duat, and the Theban necropolis was portrayed as a mountain with her cow emerging from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>From the Fourth Dynasty Hathor was the goddess most closely linked with the king, and late Old Kingdom rulers promoted her cult in the provinces to bind those regions to the court. More temples were dedicated to her than to any other Egyptian goddess. At Dendera, her oldest temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, which dates to at least the Fourth Dynasty, kings made additions across Egyptian history, and the last version, from the Ptolemaic and Roman periods, is among the best preserved of that time.</p><p>Many festivals involved drinking and dancing, such as the Festival of Drunkenness on the twentieth day of the month of Thout. In households women prayed to her for children, and the only known surviving birth brick is decorated with an image of a woman holding her child between images of Hathor. Isis increasingly overshadowed her after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, but she continued to be venerated until the early centuries CE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hathor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the centre of the city stood the temple of its goddess, the Baalat Gebal, the Lady of Byblos, whom the Egyptians identified with <b>Hathor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Each district of Egypt had its own gods, often in animal form: the falcon Horus, the jackal Anubis, the cow <b>Hathor</b>, the crocodile Sobek, the ram Khnum.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Byblos honoured Baalat Gebal, “the Lady of Byblos”, whom the Egyptians identified with <b>Hathor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>The Great Temple was dedicated to the gods Amun, Ra-Horakhty and Ptah, Egypt's three state deities of the time, as well as to the deified Ramesses, and the Small Temple to the goddess <b>Hathor</b>, personified by Nefertari.</blockquote><a href="/work/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
